<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">South Ossetia</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Tskhinvali</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">State in the South Caucasus, partially recognised and regarded by most countries as part of Georgia; held by separatists since 1992 and dependent on Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The South Ossetian Autonomous Oblast was set up by the Soviet authorities in 1922 within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Its regional council proclaimed a sovereign republic on 20 September 1990, and on 11 December 1990 the Georgian government abolished its autonomy. The 1991-1992 war followed. A referendum on 19 January 1992 approved independence, which the regional council declared on 29 May 1992; <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/georgia">Georgia</a> and almost all other states reject this.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>About 100,000 ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetians">Ossetians</a> fled the fighting, along with 23,000 ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>. A ceasefire brokered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sochi">Sochi</a> on 24 June 1992, and the region was generally peaceful until mid-2004. Its presidents have included Eduard Kokoity, Leonid Tibilov and Anatoly Bibilov. The president is elected for five years and the parliament has 34 members. The economy is critically dependent on Russian aid. The South Ossetian and Russian presidents signed an alliance and integration treaty on 18 March 2015.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In August 2008 fighting between Georgian forces and South Ossetian and Russian forces became the Russo-Georgian War, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised South Ossetia on 26 August 2008. Four UN member states now recognise it: Russia, Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. A 2017 referendum gave the names Republic of South Ossetia and State of Alania equal status. Alan Gagloev succeeded Bibilov in 2022, and Marat Kambolov has been acting president since 23 June 2026.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/south-ossetia.jpg" alt="South Ossetia" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Various · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Ossetia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tskhinvali</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eduard Kokoity, Leonid Tibilov, Anatoly Bibilov, Alan Gagloev, Marat Kambolov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodoxy</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ossetian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of South Ossetia, State of Alania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=south-ossetia" aria-label="South Ossetia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5747 675 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where South Ossetia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6067 931L6091 947"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6067 931h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6067 931h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tskhinvali. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=south-ossetia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
